invariant: INV-17
file: ghash_vector.h
catches: ghash_equiv_test
reason: the host object's GHASH reads each power of H and its halves added
  through an ordinary pointer, so the compiler loads the eight powers once
  and holds them in registers across every pass. They do not fit there
  beside a pass's blocks, and gcc and clang, on ARMv8 and on x86-64, each
  copy a power, or its halves added, to a stack slot of the function's
  own, which the wipe of ghash_state does not clear, so a later caller's
  uninitialized stack reads a function of the key. No output changes, so
  no vector notices. bin/ghash_equiv_test copies the stack below one call
  over three passes and requires no power of H and no power's halves
  added in the copy.

--- old
    const volatile ghash_vector *power = &s->powers[i].power;
    return *power;
}

static inline ghash_vector ghash_halves_at(const ghash_state *s, size_t i) {
    const volatile ghash_vector *halves = &s->powers[i].halves_added;
--- new
    const ghash_vector *power = &s->powers[i].power;
    return *power;
}

